## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key insight is that multiple reputable sources (CBS News, AP News, Ballotpedia, U.S. Senate records) consistently confirm that the U.S. Senate held its roll-call vote to confirm Amy Coney Barrett as an Associate Justice of the Supreme Court on October 26, 2020, with a final tally of 52 yeas to 48 nays. While her commission was issued and the judicial oath was administered the following day (October 27, 2020), the evidence is unequivocal regarding the date of the Senate's confirmation vote itself.

The information is sufficient to make a clear decision. The claim states Barrett "was confirmed as US Supreme Court Justice on October 26, 2020." In common and legal parlance, a Justice is considered "confirmed" when the Senate votes to approve the nomination. The evidence directly supports this event occurring on the specified date.

Therefore, the conclusion is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The U.S. Senate voted to confirm Amy Coney Barrett as an Associate Justice of the Supreme Court on October 26, 2020, with a final tally of 52-48, as consistently reported by multiple sources including [CBS News](https://www.cbsnews.com/losangeles/news/scotus-amy-coney-barrett/), the [Associated Press](https://apnews.com/article/election-2020-donald-trump-virus-outbreak-ruth-bader-ginsburg-amy-coney-barrett-82a02a618343c98b80ca2b6bf9eafe07), and [Ballotpedia](https://ballotpedia.org/Supreme_Court_vacancy,_2020). While her commission was issued and the judicial oath was administered the following day, the Senate's confirmation vote—the defining event for "confirmation"—occurred on the date specified in the claim.